{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 45, "model_name": "meta/llama-3.3-70b-instruct", "content": "Raymond III Count of Tripoli was a major nobleman in the 12th century he was the son of Raymond II Count of Tripoli and Hodierna of Jerusalem he succeeded his father as Count of Tripoli in 1152. \nThe *Early Life* section of his biography states that Raymond III was born around 1140 and was the son of Raymond II and Hodierna he was a member of the House of Toulouse and his family had a long history of involvement in the Crusades. \nRaymond III played a major role in the *Military Campaigns* of the Crusader states during his reign he was a skilled military leader and led several successful campaigns against the Muslim states of Syria. \nThe *Regency of Jerusalem* section of his biography states that Raymond III served as regent of the Kingdom of Jerusalem for a period of time during the reign of King Baldwin IV he was a powerful and influential nobleman and played a major role in the politics of the Crusader states. \nRaymond III was also involved in the *Assassination of Walter of Montferrand* section of his biography states that he was accused of being involved in the assassination of Walter of Montferrand who was a nobleman and a supporter of the King of Jerusalem. \nThe *Later Life and Death* section of his biography states that Raymond III died in 1187 during the Battle of Hattin he was a major nobleman and played a significant role in the history of the Crusader states his death was a major blow to the Crusader states and contributed to their decline. \nThe *Legacy* section of his biography states that Raymond III was remembered as a skilled military leader and a powerful nobleman he played a major role in the history of the Crusader states and his legacy continued to be felt for centuries after his death. \nRaymond III was a major figure in the *Crusades* and his biography provides valuable insights into the history of the Crusader states during the 12th century. \nThe *Family and Children* section of his biography states that Raymond III was married to Hodierna of Jerusalem and had several children his family continued to play a major role in the history of the Crusader states after his death. \nThe *Relationships with Other Nobles* section of his biography states that Raymond III had close relationships with other nobleman of the Crusader states including King Baldwin IV of Jerusalem and Bohemond III of Antioch. \nThe *Historical Significance* section of his biography states that Raymond III was a significant figure in the history of the Crusader states and his legacy continued to be felt for centuries after his death.", "logprobs": [{"token": "Ray", "logprob": -2.9802276912960224e-06}, {"token": "mond", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " III", "logprob": -1.1920928244535389e-07}, {"token": " Count", "logprob": -0.0002613918040879071}, {"token": " of", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " Trip", "logprob": -3.576278118089249e-07}, {"token": "oli", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " was", "logprob": -0.00021705655672121793}, {"token": " a", "logprob": -0.3966996371746063}, {"token": " major", "logprob": -1.2672427892684937}, {"token": " noble", "logprob": -1.996418833732605}, {"token": "man", "logprob": -0.9041976928710938}, {"token": " in", "logprob": -5.6980417866725475e-05}, {"token": " the", "logprob": -2.622600959512056e-06}, {"token": " ", "logprob": -1.295238733291626}, {"token": "12", "logprob": -1.3470558769768104e-05}, {"token": "th", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " century", "logprob": -0.048587556928396225}, {"token": " he", "logprob": -0.026318909600377083}, {"token": " was", "logprob": -0.2514566481113434}, {"token": " the", "logprob": -0.05097493529319763}, {"token": " son", "logprob": -0.018230615183711052}, {"token": " of", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " Raymond", "logprob": -1.1920858014491387e-05}, {"token": " II", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " Count", "logprob": -0.013213451020419598}, {"token": " of", "logpro